/-!
# Composition of bounds across scales, continued: Lemma 2.1, Theorem H, Lemma WC, Theorems R, R₂

A Lean 4 / Mathlib development about how scale-by-scale bounds compose in a multiscale argument.
It continues the series from Parts 1–3 (Lemma W, Proposition C, Lemma LC and a 1D chain), and its
parts are numbered 4–6. It does not import Parts 1–3: where a part uses one of their definitions
or statements, it re-defines or re-proves it. Each part was written as a separate module and is
wrapped here in its own `section`, so that its `open` commands stay local; the namespaces are
distinct, so no names clash.

| part | section | namespace | content |
|---|---|---|---|
| 4 | `Part4_SectionConsumer` | `SectionConsumer` | Lemma 2.1: seminorm domination ⇔ kernel inclusion; the multiscale weight recursion and its uniform loss bound; fibre-supported block replacement; graph connectivity and extension |
| 5 | `Part5_HullIteration` | `HullIteration` | Theorem H, hull iteration (i)–(vi); Proposition P2; examples and counterexamples |
| 6 | `Part6_ChannelComposition` | `ChannelComposition` | Lemma WC (Lemma W for channels); the loss bound and the triangular criterion; Theorems R and R₂; examples |

## Main results

* **Lemma 2.1** (Part 4). On a finite-dimensional real vector space, a seminorm `q` is bounded by
  a multiple of a seminorm `p` iff `ker p ⊆ ker q` (`seminorm_dominated_iff_ker_le`). Hence, for a
  seminorm `𝒯` and finitely many linear terms `ℓ_s`, (H1) `𝒯 ≤ Σ_s K_s |ℓ_s|` holds for some
  `K_s ≥ 0` iff every `Δ` with `ℓ_s(Δ) = 0` for all `s` has `𝒯(Δ) = 0` (`lemma_2_1`); one such
  twist `Δ` rules out (H1) for every `K` (`not_H1_of_twist`), and the finiteness of the family of
  terms cannot be dropped (`gap_example`).
* **The multiscale weight recursion** (Part 4). For the cost family `𝒞_M(a, b, c)` with level-`k`
  weights `β^(k)_j`, every coarser weight is exactly `c^{k+j}` (`beta_coarse`). Under
  `0 ≤ κ_k ≤ κ`, `0 ≤ b*_k ≤ b*`, `a ≥ 0`, `c ≥ 8` and `c > κ`, the finest weight satisfies
  `β^(k)_0 ≤ c^k (c + a b*)/(c − κ)` (`beta_zero_le_sharp`), so the loss
  `Ω_M = max_{k ≤ M} β^(k)_0 / c^k` is at most `(c + a b*)/(c − κ)` for every `M`
  (`lossRatio_le_sharp`); `c > κ` cannot be dropped (`beta_zero_ge_of_le_kappa`). The channel
  inequalities give admissibility, `N_k(R_k z) ≤ N_{k+1}(z)` (`admissible`).
* **Fibre-touched sets and extension** (Part 4). A block replacement that resamples only inside
  fibres leaves every fibre-constant term unchanged (`expect_blockUpdate`), so the block-recurrence
  step of Lemma LC holds with the fibre-touched sets (`block_recurrence_fibre`), which are never
  larger than the link-support touched sets (`mem_supportTouched_of_mem_fibreTouched`). On a graph
  with blocks and block-local linear constraints, if a set `C` separates no block it meets, the
  exterior gauge function `g` has zero gradient on the links off `C` inside those blocks, and the
  constraints of the other blocks hold for `g`, then some `g'` agrees with `g` off `C`, has zero
  gradient inside every block meeting `C`, and satisfies every constraint (`prop_2_4_core`, the
  combinatorial core of Proposition 2.4).
* **Theorem H** (Part 5). For any class of functions with a hull operator, the hull chain
  `H_0 = ‖·‖₁`, `H_{k+1} = (H_k ∘ R_k)^𝒩` is admissible and is the least admissible chain in the
  class (i) (`hullChain_admissible`, `hullChain_minimal`); it dominates the hull of the truth
  `‖T_k ·‖₁`, which dominates the truth (ii) (`chain_ge_oneShot_ge_truth`); along an invariant
  direction its loss is a product of per-step factors `g_i ≥ 1` (iii) (`H_iii`). In the
  site-additive (box) class, `H_k = d_{Λ⁽ᵏ⁾}` with the minimal weights of Lemma W, and the one-shot
  weights satisfy `|T_k|ᵀ1 ≤ Λ⁽ᵏ⁾`, with equality if every `R_i ≥ 0` (iv) (`H_iv`,
  `oneShotWeights_eq_minWeights_of_nonneg`); a left inverse `A_k` of `T_k` with
  `‖A_k‖₁→₁ ≤ 1/s_k` bounds the one-shot loss by `max_w ‖T_k e_w‖₁ / s_k` (v)
  (`oneShot_loss_chain`); and `d_{Λ⁽ᵏ⁾}(y) = ‖T_k y‖₁` for every `y` as soon as every `R_i` has
  pairwise disjoint column supports, in particular for nonnegative right inverses of positive block
  averages on partitions (vi) (`wl1_minWeights_eq_truth`, `wl1_minWeights_eq_truth_of_block`,
  `H_vi`).
* **Proposition P2** (Part 5). If `A⁻¹ > 0` entrywise, `P` is a positive block average on a
  partition with at least two blocks, the blocks are thick and `P A⁻¹ Pᵀ` is invertible, then
  `R = A⁻¹ Pᵀ (P A⁻¹ Pᵀ)⁻¹` has a negative entry (`P2_core`, `P2_formula`). Thickness cannot be
  dropped (`P2_thick_needed`), and (vi) needs the blocks to cover the fine sites
  (`vi_needs_cover`).
* **Lemma WC** (Part 6). For norms `N_{k,c}(y) = ⟨c, p_k(y)⟩` built from channel profiles: a
  transfer matrix `A_k` with `p_k(R_k z) ≤ A_k p_{k+1}(z)` makes `A_kᵀ c_k ≤ c_{k+1}` sufficient for
  admissibility when `c_k ≥ 0` and the profiles are nonnegative (a) (`stepAdmissible_of_transfer`),
  and necessary when test vectors isolate the channels and are aligned (e) (`stepAdmissible_iff`);
  under an anchor lower bound with factor `s` and channel domination `p_k(y)_r ≤ D_r p_k(y)_o`, the
  loss of the normalised chain is at most `⟨c̃_k, D⟩` (c) (`normChain_loss_bound`), uniformly in
  `k` under a triangular criterion (d) (`triangular_loss_bound`); nested channels admit no
  isolating test vectors (f)
  (`not_isolated_of_nested`); and profile domination with `λ > s` rules out a `k`-uniform loss
  constant (g) (`not_uniform_loss_of_profile_domination`). For coordinate channels Lemma WC gives
  Lemma W back (`admissible_iff`, `minimal_weights`).
* **Theorems R and R₂** (Part 6). For the norm family with a deviation channel `(I − P_k) y`,
  `P_k = R_k V_k`, at depth 1 and 2, with `V_k R_k = I`, bounds on `R_k` and the curvature `Γ_k`,
  and large enough deviation weights: the family composes iff a value-only bound holds at the first
  step and the two-term condition holds on the range of the reconstruction at every later scale
  (`thmR_family_iff`, `thmR2_family_iff`). Under the anchor lower bound `s‖z‖ ≤ ‖R_k z‖` and
  bounds on `R_k`, `V_k` and `Γ_k`, the family satisfies `N_k(y) ≤ Θ ‖T_k y‖` with a constant `Θ`
  independent of `k` (`thmR_loss_bound`, `thmR2_loss_bound`).

Every part ends with examples on concrete data in which the hypotheses of its main theorems are
discharged, so that none of them is vacuous.

## Scope

These are results about proof methods at Gaussian order (seminorm domination and the arithmetic of
a multiscale cost family, hull iteration for norm chains, and channel composition). Nothing here is
about the Yang–Mills measure. The estimates (D) (the cube estimate `D(n, α)`) and (E) (the
existence of a nontrivial continuum limit satisfying the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms with a gap in
physical units) remain open, and so does the mass gap; nothing here addresses them.

## Not formalised here

* the identification of `κ_k`, `b*_k`, the channels and the truth weights of Part 4 with the
  operators of the multiscale block-gauge section (Fact G, Lemma F, `V^(k) T_k = I`); Lemma 0′
